{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And when an ox gores, etc., et a man.</b> The reason for his saying <b>et</b> may be explained according to what they say in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 79a): if it intended to kill Shimon and killed Reuben, it is liable, for it intended to kill one who is subject to liability on its account. And this is what he means by <b>et a man</b> — to include anyone whom it intended [to kill], even though it killed another whom it did not intend. But specifically if it intended [to gore] a man; however, if it intended [to gore] an animal and killed a man, it is exempt, as we say there (Sanhedrin 78b)." "<b>And he dies.</b> Meaning, even if he died after a period of time, as long as he died on account of the goring. And so it follows from the words of Rashi (Bava Kamma 41a) in explaining the sugya of [an ox that] gored three people and they did not die — see there." "<b>And its flesh shall not be eaten.</b> And if one ate it, he receives lashes. But other forms of benefit that are inferred from his saying ״וּבַעַל הַשּׁוֹר נָקִי״ — although forbidden, one does not receive lashes for them. Therefore he did not include everything under one comprehensive category."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:09.016951433Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}